@preamble let path = import("path")
separator
Returns the path separator used by the current host as a one-character string.
This is the only operation in path that directly crosses the HAL.

separator()

type(path.separator())    # :string
===
cwd
Returns the working directory owned by the current Aiki runtime. This composes
system.cwd(); it does not query a second host facility.

cwd()

type(path.cwd())    # :string
===
join
@unchecked
Joins two path components with the host separator. If one component already
provides the separator at the join point, it is not duplicated. This is an
ordinary Aiki string operation.

join(left, right)

path.join("one", "two")
===
dir
@unchecked
Returns everything before the final host separator. A path with no separator
has an empty directory part. The root directory is returned as the separator.

This is an ordinary Aiki string operation.

dir(path)

path.dir("one/file.ai")
===
base
Returns everything after the final host separator. If there is no separator,
returns the path unchanged.

This is an ordinary Aiki string operation.

base(path)

path.base("one/file.ai")    # "file.ai"
===
ext
Returns the final basename extension, including the leading dot, or "" when
there is no dot in the basename.

This is an ordinary Aiki string operation.

ext(path)

path.ext("one/file.ai")    # ".ai"
===
normalize
@unchecked
Normalizes a path using the host separator. Repeated separators and "."
components are removed. A ".." component removes the preceding ordinary
component when one exists. Leading ".." components are retained for relative
paths and cannot move an absolute path above its root.

An empty normalized relative path is returned as ".". This is an ordinary
Aiki computation; no host path-cleaning routine defines these semantics.

normalize(path)

path.normalize("one/./two/../three")
===
is_absolute
Returns true when path begins with the current host separator. This is the
portable Aiki contract for absolute paths in the initial path library.

is_absolute(path)

path.is_absolute(path.separator() + "tmp")    # true
